## Step 2

```{r}
# Recategorize diagnosis into binary status: EAOC vs non-EAOC
endometriosis_copy$eaoc_status <- factor(
  ifelse(endometriosis_copy$diagnosis_group == "EAOC", "EAOC", "non-EAOC")
)

# Set "Stage I" as the reference category for disease_stage
endometriosis_copy$disease_stage <- relevel(
  factor(endometriosis_copy$disease_stage), 
  ref = "Stage I"  
)


# Fit binary logistic regression model to estimate odds of belonging to the EAOC group
#To evaluate the association between disease progression and malignant transformation, a binary logistic regression model (glm with family = binomial) was fitted using eaoc_status as the binary response variable ($1 = \text{EAOC}$, $0 = \text{Non-EAOC}$) and disease_stage as the primary categorical/ordinal predictor.

logit_model <- glm(
  eaoc_status ~ disease_stage, 
  data = endometriosis_copy, 
  family = binomial
)


# Extract Odds Ratios (OR) and calculate 95% Confidence Intervals (CI)
or_matrix <- exp(cbind(OR = coef(logit_model), confint(logit_model)))
print(or_matrix)

```

### Interpretation

Binary logistic regression evaluates the odds of having EAOC vs non-EAOC
across disease stages, setting Stage I as the baseline reference
category. Exponentiating the regression coefficients ($\text{e}^\beta$)
yields Odds Ratios (OR) with $95\%$ Confidence Intervals (CI).
Results:The model outputs extreme, unstable estimates for advanced
stages ($\text{OR} \approx 0$, upper bounds reaching Inf or 2.3e+42, and
NA for standard errors). This behavior occurs due to quasi-complete
separation: as shown in the Step 1 contingency table, Stages III and IV
contain exclusively EAOC cases ($100\%$) and zero non-EAOC cases.
Because there are no non-EAOC controls in advanced stages, the maximum
likelihood estimation (MLE) algorithm fails to converge properly for
these parameters, leading to numerical instability and non-calculable
confidence limits. While the uncorrected logistic regression model
breaks down numerically due to empty cells, the underlying biological
pattern is unambiguous: advanced disease stages (III and IV) are
overwhelmingly associated with malignant EAOC status compared to Stage
I.

