## Step 3

```{r}
# Create binary stage variable: Early (Stage I, Stage II) vs Advanced (Stage III, Stage IV)
endometriosis_copy$stage_binary <- factor(
  ifelse(endometriosis_copy$disease_stage %in% c("Stage I", "Stage II"), "Early (I-II)", "Advanced (III-IV)")
) 

# Create 2x2 Contingency Table (Stage Binary vs EAOC Status)
table_2x2 <- table(endometriosis_copy$stage_binary, endometriosis_copy$eaoc_status)
print("--- 2x2 Contingency Table ---")
print(table_2x2)

# Chi-squared test with Yates' continuity correction
chisq_yates <- chisq.test(table_2x2, correct = TRUE)
print("--- Chi-squared Test with Yates' Correction ---")
print(chisq_yates)
```

### Interpretation

*The* $2 \times 2$ contingency table yields 9 EAOC cases and 0 non-EAOC
cases in the Advanced stage (III-IV), compared to 19 EAOC cases and 284
non-EAOC cases in the Early stage (I-II). Pearson's Chi-squared test
with Yates' continuity correction yields $\chi^2 = 82.873$
($df = 1, p < 2.2 \times 10^{-16}$). Yates' Continuity Correction
applied specifically to $2 \times 2$ contingency tables, this correction
subtracts $0.5$ from the absolute difference between observed and
expected frequencies ($\vert{}O - E\vert{} - 0.5$). It adjusts for
approximating a discrete binomial distribution with a continuous
Chi-squared distribution, yielding a more conservative $p$-value to
prevent Type I error (false positives). R displays a warning
("Chi-squared approximation may be incorrect") because one cell in the
$2 \times 2$ table has an observed frequency of $0$ (Advanced non-EAOC),
leading to low expected frequencies in that cell. Despite the
statistical warning, the association between advanced stage disease and
malignant EAOC status remains extremely strong ($p < 0.001$). $100\%$ of
patients presenting with advanced disease (III-IV) belong to the EAOC
malignant group, reinforcing that advanced clinical stages represent a
severe, high-risk phenotypes.

