## Final Comment for Question 1

This multi-step biostatistical analysis provides key insights into risk
stratification for Endometriosis-Associated Ovarian Cancer (EAOC):

-   Clinical Staging and Malignancy: disease stage shows an
    exceptionally strong association with diagnosis status
    ($p < 0.001$). Early stages (I-II) are predominantly composed of
    benign endometriosis and atypical lesions, whereas advanced stages
    (III-IV) consist exclusively of EAOC cases.

-   Methodological Considerations: standard logistic regression models
    encounter quasi-complete separation due to zero control cases in
    advanced stages. Grouping stages into binary categories (early vs
    advanced) and applying Yates' continuity correction
    ($\chi^2 = 82.873, p < 2.2 \times 10^{-16}$) resolves estimation
    issues while confirming a statistically significant risk shift
    toward malignancy in advanced stages.

-   Genomic Biomarkers: isolated copy number loss of the DVL1 gene
    showed no statistically significant association with diagnostic
    groups ($p = 0.4905$). This indicates that DVL1 CNV loss alone is
    insufficient as a standalone biomarker for EAOC transformation and
    must be analyzed alongside broader multi-omic profiles.

