# Question 2

## Assigned Exercise

> Build prognostic models for overall survival in patients affected by
> endometriosis and endometriosis-associated ovarian cancer using Cox
> regression.

1.  Fit a multivariable Cox proportional hazards model including
    clinical predictors such as age at diagnosis, ethnicity, diagnosis
    group (Endometriosis, Atypical endometriosis, EAOC), and relevant
    disease characteristics (bilateral disease). Estimate hazard ratios
    for each prognostic factor.

2.  Test the proportional hazards assumption using Schoenfeld residuals
    or other graphical diagnostics, clearly justifying your choice.

3.  Based on the proportional hazards assumption results, extend the Cox
    model by introducing a stratification according to bilateral disease
    showing evidence of non-proportional / proportional hazards.
    Interpret the results and compare them with the standard Cox model.

4.  Visualize the effect of prognostic factors using adjusted survival
    curves.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Step 1

```{r}
# Fit Multivariable Cox Proportional Hazards Model
# Time variable: time_years, Event variable: status (0=alive/censored, 1=death)
cox_model <- coxph( Surv(time_years, status) ~ age + factor(ethnicity) + factor(family_history) + BMI + factor(diagnosis_group) + factor(bilateral_disease), 
  data = endometriosis_copy)

# Summary of Cox Model to extract Hazard Ratios (HR) and 95% Confidence Intervals
summary(cox_model)
```

### Interpretation

The multivariable Cox proportional hazards model demonstrated robust
overall statistical significance across all tests: likelihood Ratio test
($39.32$ on $9\text{ df}, p = 1 \times 10^{-5}$), Wald test ($44.26$ on
$9\text{ df}, p = 1 \times 10^{-6}$), and Score log-rank test ($52.73$
on $9\text{ df}, p = 3 \times 10^{-8}$). Incorporating body mass index
(BMI) improved the model's predictive discrimination, increasing the
Concordance index ($C$-index) to $0.719$ ($\text{SE} = 0.037$).The
estimated Hazard Ratios ($\text{HR} = \exp(\beta)$) for all prognostic
factors are located in the model output under the exp(coef) column, with
their corresponding $95\%$ confidence intervals provided in the lower
section under lower .95 and upper .95.

Evaluation of Prognostic Factors:

-   Diagnosis Group (EAOC): EAOC it's a major negative prognostic
    factor. Patients diagnosed with EAOC exhibited a nearly 3.4-fold
    higher hazard of death compared to the baseline group of atypical
    endometriosis. Benign endometriosis showed no significant survival
    difference relative to baseline ($\text{HR} = 0.95$,
    $95\%\text{ CI } [0.44, 2.05]$, $p = 0.900$).
-   Body Mass Index (BMI): BMI emerged as a statistically significant
    predictor ($\text{HR} = 1.10$, $95\%\text{ CI } [1.03, 1.16]$,
    $p = 0.00218$). Each 1-unit increase in BMI was associated with a
    $9.6\%$ incremental increase in mortality hazard, highlighting the
    adverse prognostic contribution of metabolic factors in this cohort.
-   Other Clinical Covariates: Age at diagnosis
    ($\text{HR} = 1.02, p = 0.177$), family history of cancer
    ($\text{HR} = 0.88, p = 0.681$), presence of bilateral disease
    ($\text{HR} = 0.71, p = 0.255$), and ethnic subgroups ($p > 0.05$
    across all levels relative to Asian baseline) did not show
    statistically significant associations with overall survival.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Step 2

```{r}
# Test Proportional Hazards (PH) Assumption using Schoenfeld Residuals
ph_test <- cox.zph(cox_model)
print(ph_test)

# Plot Schoenfeld Residuals for graphical diagnosis
ggcoxzph(ph_test)
```

### Interpretation

The global Schoenfeld test yields a $p$-value of $0.4777$, indicating
that the model as a whole does not exhibit severe global
non-proportionality.

Most predictors satisfy the proportional hazards (PH) assumption: age
($p = 0.8017$), ethnicity ($p = 0.6045$), BMI ($p = 0.6981$),
diagnosis_group ($p = 0.8737$), and bilateral_disease ($p = 0.2466$).

Violation Detected: The covariate family_history displays a
statistically significant violation of the proportional hazards
assumption ($p = 0.0447$). The residual plot reveals a time-dependent
regression coefficient ($\beta(t)$), where the effect of family history
varies over follow-up duration.

Visual inspection of the Schoenfeld residual plots confirms that while
primary clinical predictors (such as diagnosis_group and BMI) show flat
horizontal smoothing curves ($\beta(t) \approx \text{constant}$),
family_history and bilateral_disease display subtle non-linear
trajectories over time. Detecting non-proportional hazards in background
clinical covariates justifies extending the standard multivariable Cox
framework to a stratified Cox model in Step 3. Stratifying by disease
characteristics (bilateral_disease) accounts for underlying baseline
hazard heterogeneity across subgroups, ensuring unbiased and stable
Hazard Ratio estimates for key predictors.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Step 3

```{r}
# Fit Stratified Cox Proportional Hazards Model by bilateral_disease
strat_cox_model <- coxph(
  Surv(time_years, status) ~ age + factor(ethnicity) + factor(diagnosis_group) + 
    factor(family_history) + BMI + strata(bilateral_disease), 
  data = endometriosis_copy
)

# Summary of Stratified Cox Model
summary(strat_cox_model)

# Survival curves by stratification variable (bilateral_disease)
fit_strat <- survfit(strat_cox_model)

# Graphical visualization of the two strata baseline curves
ggsurvplot(
  fit_strat,
  data = endometriosis_copy,
  conf.int = FALSE,
  legend.title = "Bilateral Disease",
  legend.labs = c("No (0)", "Yes (1)"),
  xlab = "Time (years)",
  ylab = "Survival Probability",
  ggtheme = theme_minimal(),
  title = "Baseline Survival Probabilities Stratified by Bilateral Disease"
)
# Likelihood Ratio / AIC comparison between models
#Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) is a statistical metric used to compare and select the best model among a set of candidates applied to the same dataset (Lower is Better)
AIC(cox_model, strat_cox_model)
```

### Interpretation

Stratifying by bilateral_disease allows the baseline hazard function
$\lambda_0(t)$ to vary freely across strata while estimating shared
regression coefficients for the remaining covariates. The overall
stratified specification is highly significant ($p = 5 \times 10^{-6}$
on Likelihood Ratio test) with a Concordance index of $C = 0.698$
($\text{SE} = 0.043$).

Prognostic Factor Estimates:

-   Diagnosis Group (EAOC): confirmed as a primary independent risk
    factor. Patients with EAOC exhibit a statistically significant
    $3.24$-fold increase in mortality hazard relative to atypical
    endometriosis ($\text{HR} = 3.24$, $95\%\text{ CI } [1.47, 7.11]$,
    $p = 0.00342$).

-   Body Mass Index (BMI): Maintains independent prognostic significance
    ($\text{HR} = 1.09$, $95\%\text{ CI } [1.03, 1.16]$, $p = 0.00377$),
    indicating a $9.1\%$ relative increase in mortality risk per unit
    increase in BMI.

-   Other Covariates: Age ($\text{HR} = 1.02, p = 0.291$), family
    history ($\text{HR} = 0.95, p = 0.877$), benign endometriosis
    ($\text{HR} = 0.84, p = 0.651$), and ethnicity categories
    ($p > 0.05$ across all levels relative to Asian baseline) show no
    statistically significant associations with overall survival.

Graphical Diagnosis of Stratified Strata: The baseline survival curves
for bilateral disease strata ($0 = \text{Unilateral}$,
$1 = \text{Bilateral}$) exhibit clear temporal divergence, intersecting
around 6–7 years before dropping sharply for the unilateral cohort after
year 9. This crossing of survival trajectories visually confirms
non-proportional hazard behavior between disease extent groups. Allowing
separate stratum-specific baseline hazard functions ($\lambda_{0s}(t)$)
resolves this baseline hazard heterogeneity, directly driving the
substantial model fit improvement ($\Delta\text{AIC} = 76.26$) and
ensuring unbiased hazard ratio estimates for key predictors like EAOC
status and BMI.\*

